---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Banner from "../components/Banner.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Peter Jones | Homepage">
  <Banner src="/images/banners/CanadianFlagReflection.jpg" />
  <div class="content fade-in visible">
    <h1 style="display:none">Home Page</h1>

    <section class="section section-one">
      <h2>Welcome</h2>
      <p>Thank you for visiting my website.</p>
      <p>
        I am a Front End Web Developer in Toronto and I have been building
        websites for more than 10 years. My background is making websites with
        HTML, CSS and JavaScript from Photoshop mockups. I have worked with PHP,
        mySql, Wordpress, jQuery and many other technologies. When mobile
        devices arrived, I learned responsive web design with fluid grids as
        well as the importance of web standards, cross browser compatibility and
        accessibility.
      </p>
      <p>
        With the arrival of task managers such as Gulp and Grunt, minifying CSS
        and Javascript as well as optimizing images was no longer a chore and
        the benefits of faster load times became obvious using these techniques.
        Flexbox and CSS Grid gave us the ability to design web pages in ways
        that we could never have imagined. At any rate, the industry has moved
        on and new technologies have appeared, and so has the way sites like
        this one get built.
      </p>
      <p>
        Staying on top of the latest technologies and techniques is challenging
        but I want to keep building websites using the latest and greatest. This
        site is built with Astro — content ships as plain static HTML, and React
        is used only for the handful of pages that are genuinely interactive.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="section section-two">
      <div class="left">
        <h3>So what&apos;s here?</h3>
        <p>
          Most of this site is static — the content doesn't change per visitor,
          so it's rendered once at build time instead of shipping a JavaScript
          framework to reconstruct it in your browser.
        </p>
        <p>
          The interactive project demos under <a href="/projects">Projects</a>
          are still built with React, but each one loads independently, only on the
          page that needs it — the homepage you're reading right now ships no framework
          JavaScript at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          I have tried to explain more on this in the Code Snippets Section.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="right"></div>
    </section>
    <section class="section section-three">
      <div class="left"></div>
      <div class="right">
        <h3>Code Snippets Section</h3>
        <p>
          In this section I have converted some past side projects written in
          HTML, CSS and Javascript.
        </p>
        <p>
          The original code is shown in the syntax highlighting sections -
          usually one section for each. For the syntax highlighting I used Prism
          which is a lightweight, extensible syntax highlighter. Check out the <a
            href="https://prismjs.com/"
            target="_new">Prism website</a
          > to learn more.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want to see how the original snippets worked, you can
          copy/paste from the snippets into separate files (i.e. index.html,
          styles.css and scripts.js) and run them in a browser.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is a short explanation as to what was required to convert the
          snippet for use with React at the top of each snippet&apos;s page.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section section-four">
      <div class="left">
        <h3>News Feeds Section</h3>
        <p>I have always added a news feeds section to my site.</p>
        <p>
          In React we can use the <code>Fetch API</code> to pull data and display
          it on our site.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the news feed on this site I'm pulling RSS feeds from <a
            href="https://www.cbc.ca/rss/"
            target="_new">CBC</a
          >. Thanks <a href="https://www.cbc.ca/" target="_new">CBC!</a> Since RSS
          feeds don't allow cross-origin requests from the browser, a small server
          endpoint on this site proxies each feed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="right"></div>
    </section>
    <section class="section section-five">
      <div class="left"></div>
      <div class="right">
        <h3>Contact Form Section</h3>
        <p>Every website should have a contact form, even a simple one.</p>
        <p>
          This site is self-hosted, so the contact form posts to a small API
          endpoint on this same server rather than relying on a third-party
          forms service.
        </p>
        <p>
          Go ahead - <a href="/contact">send me a message</a>. I promise to get
          back to you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Scoped to the five named homepage sections below, rather than a bare */
  /* `section {}` selector, which applied site-wide to every <section> on */
  /* every page (e.g. each project's CodeBlocks <section class="code">). */
  /* Currently harmless in practice — 1.5 matches the syntax highlighter's */
  /* own inline line-height, which wins regardless — but same leak pattern */
  /* as the bare `span` rule in rollup-counter.css, so scoped on principle. */
  .section-one,
  .section-two,
  .section-three,
  .section-four,
  .section-five {
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .section-one {
    background-color: transparent;
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }
  .left {
    width: 50%;
  }
  .right {
    width: 50%;
  }
  .section-two {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
  }
  .section-two .left {
    padding: 60px;
    min-height: 400px;
  }
  .section-two .right {
    background-image: url('/images/banners/WebDev.jpg');
    background-size: cover;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    background-position: top center;
    min-height: 200px;
    height: auto;
  }
  .section-three {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
  }
  .section-three .left {
    background-image: url('/images/banners/Keyboard.jpg');
    background-size: cover;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    background-position: top center;
    min-height: 200px;
  }
  .section-three .right {
    padding: 60px;
    min-height: 400px;
  }
  .section-four {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
  }
  .section-four .left {
    padding: 60px;
    min-height: 400px;
  }
  .section-four .right {
    background-image: url('/images/banners/News.jpg');
    background-size: cover;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    background-position: top center;
    min-height: 200px;
    height: auto;
  }
  .section-five {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }
  .section-five .left {
    background-image: url('/images/banners/People.jpg');
    background-size: cover;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    background-position: top center;
    min-height: 200px;
    height: auto;
  }
  .section-five .right {
    padding: 60px;
    min-height: 400px;
  }

  @media only screen and (max-width: 1024px) {
    .left {
      width: 100%;
    }
    .right {
      width: 100%;
    }
    .section-one {
      padding: 10px;
    }
    .section-two {
      flex-direction: column-reverse;
    }
    .section-two .left {
      padding: 10px;
      min-height: 200px;
    }
    .section-three .right {
      padding: 10px;
      min-height: 200px;
    }
    .section-four {
      flex-direction: column-reverse;
    }
    .section-four .left {
      padding: 10px;
      min-height: 200px;
    }
    .section-five .right {
      padding: 10px;
      min-height: 200px;
    }
  }
</style>
